7] [17592186410194 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1071 "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410195 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1071 "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410196 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186410199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d7e6a3a-4e90-51f7-9e6d-1f36e15dea08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3781c4aa-7e7b-5b51-9af4-d354711b0091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80ab5b9e-fd44-5cd4-b555-7638c14ed974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410202 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218641020